name: disks and filesystems
boss: Phantom
flavor: Every privilege is a door I already opened.
brief: A disk is a block device. A filesystem is a structure written onto it.
brief: mount attaches that structure somewhere in the tree; until you mount
brief: it, a disk is just bytes.
brief:
brief: On this machine the root is a read-only squashfs verified block by block,
brief: and the host's own drives are deliberately invisible -- there are no
brief: NVMe or SATA drivers compiled in. What you can see is the stick you
brief: booted from and anything on USB.
brief:
brief: -s here means summarize the whole tree into one line -- not silent, and
brief: not a delimiter filter.
---
q: {how full are this machine's disks -- show free space on each filesystem|how much free space is left on the mounted filesystems|report the free space on every mounted filesystem}
a: df
out: 1K-blocks
ref: df
teach: df
hint: df alone reports every mounted filesystem, one line each
---
q: %show% free space in a human readable form
a: df -h
out: [0-9]+\.[0-9]+[KMGT]
ref: df
teach: df -h
hint: -h scales the numbers to k/m/g instead of raw block counts
---
q: %show% free space in megabytes
a: df -m
out: 1M-blocks
ref: df
teach: df -m
hint: -m forces megabytes specifically, no auto-scaling
---
q: space free but nothing writable -- %show% inode usage, not blocks
a: df -i
out: Inodes
ref: df
teach: df -i
hint: -i swaps the space columns for inode counts and percent-used
---
q: df skipped something -- %show% free space including pseudo-filesystems
a: df -a
out: devpts|sysfs
ref: df
teach: df -a
hint: -a includes filesystems df otherwise skips, like proc and tmpfs
---
q: mounted filesystems -- %show% free space with the type in a column
a: df -T
out: Filesystem +Type
ref: df
teach: df -T
hint: -T adds a column naming each filesystem's type
---
q: is the read-only root itself full -- %show% free space just for squashfs
a: df -t squashfs
why: root
ref: df
teach: df -t
hint: -t filters the listing down to one filesystem type
---
