setup: p3 is the state partition -- the only thing on this stick that survives
setup: a reboot, and the only place a key can live. It is encrypted, and it is
setup: also authenticated, which is a different property and the harder one.
q: {write a LUKS2 header on /dev/sda3, encrypted and authenticated|format /dev/sda3 with a LUKS2 header, encrypted and authenticated|put a new LUKS2 header on /dev/sda3, integrity checked too}
a: cryptsetup luksFormat --type luks2 --integrity hmac-sha256 /dev/sda3
a: cryptsetup luksFormat --integrity hmac-sha256 --type luks2 /dev/sda3
why: root
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup --type
teach: cryptsetup --integrity
hint: encryption stops them reading it; integrity stops them changing it
---
q: {unlock /dev/sda3 and expose it as /dev/mapper/state|open /dev/sda3 and map it as /dev/mapper/state|decrypt /dev/sda3, exposing it under /dev/mapper as state}
a: cryptsetup open /dev/sda3 state
why: root
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup
hint: open maps the decrypted device under /dev/mapper by the name you give
---
q: {unlock /dev/sda3 as state, allowing no writes|open /dev/sda3 read-only as state, no writes through it|decrypt /dev/sda3 as state, permitting no writes}
a: cryptsetup open --readonly /dev/sda3 state
why: root
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup --readonly
hint: --readonly maps the device without allowing writes through it
---
q: {lock /dev/sda3 again by removing the state mapping|close the state mapping and re-lock the device|tear down /dev/mapper/state and lock /dev/sda3 again}
a: cryptsetup close state
why: root
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup
hint: close tears down the /dev/mapper entry and re-locks the device
---
q: {is state currently open, and with what cipher|check whether state is open, and which cipher it is using|report state's current status and cipher}
a: cryptsetup status state
why: root
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup
hint: status reports whether it's active and which cipher it's using
---
q: %show% the LUKS header of /dev/sda3 -- cipher, key slots and kdf
a: cryptsetup luksDump /dev/sda3
why: root
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup
hint: luksDump reads the header itself -- it never touches the encrypted data
---
q: {format /dev/sda3 choosing the cipher explicitly|format /dev/sda3, naming the cipher instead of taking the default|choose the cipher yourself while formatting /dev/sda3}
a: cryptsetup luksFormat --cipher aes-xts-plain64 /dev/sda3
why: root
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup --cipher
hint: --cipher names the cipher instead of taking cryptsetup's default
---
q: {format /dev/sda3 with a 512 bit key|format /dev/sda3 using a 512 bit key|choose a 512 bit key while formatting /dev/sda3}
a: cryptsetup luksFormat --key-size 512 /dev/sda3
why: root
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup --key-size
hint: --key-size is in bits, and must match what the cipher supports
---
q: {format /dev/sda3 choosing pbkdf2 over the argon2id default|format /dev/sda3 with pbkdf2 instead of the argon2id default|swap /dev/sda3's key derivation to pbkdf2 while formatting}
a: cryptsetup luksFormat --pbkdf pbkdf2 /dev/sda3
why: root
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup --pbkdf
hint: --pbkdf picks the key derivation function used to slow down password guessing
---
q: {format /dev/sda3 with no prompt or confirmation, for a script|format /dev/sda3 with no confirmation prompt, suitable for a script|skip the are-you-sure prompt formatting /dev/sda3, for a script}
a: cryptsetup luksFormat --batch-mode /dev/sda3
why: root
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup --batch-mode
hint: --batch-mode skips the are-you-sure prompt, for scripting
---
q: {open /dev/sda3 as state, narrating what cryptsetup is doing|open /dev/sda3 as state while narrating each step out loud|unlock /dev/sda3 as state with verbose output on each step}
a: cryptsetup open --verbose /dev/sda3 state
why: root
ref: cryptsetup
teach: cryptsetup --verbose
hint: --verbose narrates what cryptsetup is doing as it opens the device
---
q: {mount the unlocked state device with nothing on it able to run|mount /dev/mapper/state so nothing on it can execute|attach the decrypted state device with execution disabled}
a: mount -o nosuid,nodev,noexec /dev/mapper/state /state
a: mount -o noexec,nodev,nosuid /dev/mapper/state /state
why: root
ref: mount
teach: mount -o
hint: same nosuid,nodev,noexec trio, now applied to the decrypted volume
---
setup: A disk someone hands you is untrusted input, and a filesystem driver is
setup: a parser that runs it in your kernel. The safest read of a disk you do
setup: not trust is dd at the block level, where no parser runs at all.
q: {image @txt@ (standing in for /dev/sda) to disk.img, byte for byte|make an image of @txt@ (stands in for /dev/sda) as disk.img|copy @txt@, standing in for /dev/sda, to disk.img byte for byte}
a: dd if=@txt@ of=disk.img
state: exists:disk.img
ref: dd
teach: dd
hint: no mount, no parser -- just the bytes. this is the forensically safe read
---
q: {mount a fat32 usb stick read-only, with nothing on it able to run|mount a fat32 usb stick read-only, with execution disabled|attach a fat32 usb stick read-only and let nothing on it run}
a: mount -t vfat -o ro,nosuid,nodev,noexec /dev/sda1 /mnt
a: mount -t vfat -o noexec,nodev,nosuid,ro /dev/sda1 /mnt
why: root
ref: mount
teach: mount -t
hint: ro, and noexec, because you do not trust what is on it
---
q: {mount an exfat sd card read-only and safely|safely mount an exfat sd card read-only|attach an exfat sd card read-only, locked down the same way}
a: mount -t exfat -o ro,nosuid,nodev,noexec /dev/sda1 /mnt
a: mount -t exfat -o noexec,nodev,nosuid,ro /dev/sda1 /mnt
why: root
ref: mount
teach: mount -t
hint: same ro,nosuid,nodev,noexec pattern, this time for exfat
---
q: {mount a windows ntfs disk read-only|mount a windows ntfs disk allowing no writes|attach an ntfs disk in read-only mode}
a: mount -t ntfs3 -o ro,nosuid,nodev,noexec /dev/sda2 /mnt
a: mount -t ntfs3 -o noexec,nodev,nosuid,ro /dev/sda2 /mnt
why: root
ref: mount
teach: mount -t
hint: read only -- xos will not write ntfs, by design
---
q: {loop-mount an iso image read-only without burning it|mount an iso image read-only through a loop device, no burning required|attach an iso image as a read-only loop mount}
